import{_ as t,c as a,o,ae as s}from"./chunks/framework.CliRSA9F.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"RSS Feed Optimization: The Technical Foundation of Podcast SEO","description":"Review how podcast RSS feeds support distribution and discovery, which feed elements platforms actually require, and where to look for feed problems.","frontmatter":{"description":"Review how podcast RSS feeds support distribution and discovery, which feed elements platforms actually require, and where to look for feed problems."},"headers":[],"relativePath":"academy/rss-feed-optimization-the-technical-foundation-of-podcast-seo.md","filePath":"academy/rss-feed-optimization-the-technical-foundation-of-podcast-seo.md"}'),r={name:"academy/rss-feed-optimization-the-technical-foundation-of-podcast-seo.md"};function i(n,e,d,c,l,h){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="rss-feed-optimization-the-technical-foundation-of-podcast-seo" tabindex="-1">RSS Feed Optimization: The Technical Foundation of Podcast SEO <a class="header-anchor" href="#rss-feed-optimization-the-technical-foundation-of-podcast-seo" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;RSS Feed Optimization: The Technical Foundation of Podcast SEO&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Your RSS feed is the invisible engine behind your podcast&#39;s distribution. Listeners never see it, but every podcast app reads it to learn what your show is called, who makes it, what each episode is about, and where to fetch the audio. If the feed is wrong, nothing downstream — titles, descriptions, artwork, categories — can be right.</p><p>It helps to keep two things separate as you read this article:</p><ul><li><strong>Requirements</strong> are what a platform needs in order to list and play your show at all. Fail them and episodes go missing or the feed is rejected.</li><li><strong>Discovery</strong> is how well the <em>content</em> in your feed (titles, author, descriptions) matches what people search for. That&#39;s ordinary podcast SEO, delivered through the feed.</li></ul><p>Most &quot;RSS optimization&quot; advice blurs these into a story about platforms &quot;rewarding&quot; technically clean feeds with better rankings. No platform documents anything like that. What they do document is what they require, and what metadata their search reads. This article sticks to those two things.</p><h2 id="what-platforms-actually-require" tabindex="-1">What Platforms Actually Require <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-platforms-actually-require" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What Platforms Actually Require&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Apple&#39;s published feed requirements are a good baseline because most other directories accept anything Apple accepts. Source: <a href="https://podcasters.apple.com/support/823-podcast-requirements" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Apple Podcasts RSS feed requirements</a>.</p><ul><li><strong>A valid RSS 2.0 feed</strong> with the iTunes namespace, publicly reachable (no password), served over HTTPS by a host that supports HTTP HEAD and byte-range requests so apps can stream and seek.</li><li><strong>A stable, unique <code>guid</code> for every episode</strong> that never changes. Change it and apps treat the episode as new (duplicate episodes, lost play history).</li><li><strong>A valid <code>enclosure</code></strong> for every episode with the media URL, byte length, and MIME type. ASCII-only filenames and URLs.</li><li><strong>Correct date formatting</strong> (RFC 2822) and properly escaped XML entities.</li><li><strong>Artwork</strong> in JPEG or PNG, square, between 1400×1400 and 3000×3000 pixels.</li><li><strong>Show-level fields</strong>: title, description, language, at least one <code>itunes:category</code>, <code>itunes:explicit</code>, <code>itunes:author</code>, and artwork.</li></ul><p>Your hosting provider generates all of this for you. Your job is to fill in the fields well and to notice when something breaks. PodSEO&#39;s <a href="/features/distribution-health.html">Distribution Health</a> page runs the pass/fail checklist for you, shows errors and warnings, and times each redirect hop when your feed is fetched.</p><h2 id="show-level-metadata-your-feed-s-core-identity" tabindex="-1">Show-Level Metadata: Your Feed&#39;s Core Identity <a class="header-anchor" href="#show-level-metadata-your-feed-s-core-identity" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Show-Level Metadata: Your Feed&#39;s Core Identity&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The show-level fields are what platform search reads first, so they carry the most discovery weight.</p><p><strong>Title (<code>&lt;title&gt;</code>):</strong> Apple names the show name as a search input and asks that it be specific and unique, not generic or too similar to an existing show. Spotify suggests 3–4 words with a searchable keyword. &quot;Small Business Marketing Strategies&quot; beats &quot;Marketing Podcast.&quot; Sources: <a href="https://podcasters.apple.com/support/3686-search-on-apple-podcasts" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Apple: How Search works</a>, <a href="https://creators.spotify.com/resources/grow/podcast-seo" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Spotify: Words matter</a>.</p><p><strong>Author (<code>&lt;itunes:author&gt;</code>):</strong> Often ignored, but in PodSEO&#39;s ranking observations the author field appears to have a disproportionate effect on Apple Podcasts search, where a match on the author behaves much like a match on an artist name in music search (Apple publishes no weights, so test it on your own keywords). Use a real, descriptive author string — your name or brand plus a short topical qualifier — rather than a bare studio name. The <a href="/features/planner.html">Planner</a> scores author alignment alongside title and description.</p><p><strong>Description (<code>&lt;description&gt;</code>):</strong> Spotify shows the first 250 characters before expansion and pulls them into search snippets, so lead with your core topic, then secondary topics and guest types, in full sentences. Apple&#39;s search documentation doesn&#39;t list the description as a search input, so treat it as conversion copy on Apple and as searchable text on Spotify and the web. Write it for both.</p><p>A note on <code>itunes:summary</code>: some older guides claim Apple gives this tag special ranking weight. Apple&#39;s current feed guidance is built around the standard <code>&lt;description&gt;</code> element, and <code>itunes:summary</code> is a legacy field that many hosts no longer populate. Don&#39;t rely on it; make sure your <code>&lt;description&gt;</code> is complete.</p><p><strong>Category (<code>&lt;itunes:category&gt;</code>):</strong> Categories drive chart placement and browse, not keyword search. Choose the category where your show can realistically chart and where your ideal listeners browse; a business show may be better placed in &quot;Entrepreneurship&quot; than in the crowded top-level &quot;Business.&quot; <a href="/features/all-charts.html">All Charts</a> shows what&#39;s charting in each category and country.</p><p><strong>Language (<code>&lt;language&gt;</code>):</strong> Must match the language of the show. Platforms use it to decide which markets to surface you in; a wrong value quietly limits reach.</p><h2 id="episode-level-metadata-multiple-discovery-entry-points" tabindex="-1">Episode-Level Metadata: Multiple Discovery Entry Points <a class="header-anchor" href="#episode-level-metadata-multiple-discovery-entry-points" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Episode-Level Metadata: Multiple Discovery Entry Points&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Each episode is a new chance to rank for a specific search.</p><p><strong>Episode titles:</strong> Apple names episode titles as a search input and asks creators to avoid emojis and repeated titles. Spotify asks for titles that mirror what a listener would type. A reliable pattern is <em>keyword + specific angle</em>: &quot;Email Marketing Automation: Doubling Revenue Without Hiring.&quot; Avoid inside jokes and vague creative titles, and don&#39;t lead with the episode number.</p><p><strong>Episode descriptions:</strong> Spotify recommends 3–4 specific sentences naming guests, exact topics, and the questions answered, with the searchable content in the first 250 characters; don&#39;t restate the title. Timestamps, resources, and guest links are fine after that opening. There&#39;s no documented &quot;ideal word count&quot;; specificity matters more than length.</p><p><strong>Transcripts and chapters:</strong> Spotify indexes automatic transcripts and chapter labels for eligible episodes, and Podcasting 2.0 <code>&lt;podcast:transcript&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;podcast:chapters&gt;</code> tags let other apps show them. Check that names and key terms are spelled correctly in the transcript; Distribution Health reports which Podcast 2.0 tags your feed carries.</p><h2 id="technical-hygiene-what-breaks-distribution" tabindex="-1">Technical Hygiene: What Breaks Distribution <a class="header-anchor" href="#technical-hygiene-what-breaks-distribution" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Technical Hygiene: What Breaks Distribution&quot;">​</a></h2><p>These are the problems that actually cost creators episodes and listeners. None of them is a &quot;ranking penalty&quot; — they are failures to be listed or played at all.</p><p><strong>Malformed XML.</strong> One unescaped ampersand can make a whole feed unparseable, and some apps will silently keep the last good copy while others show nothing. Distribution Health flags Well-Formed vs. Malformed and lists the specific errors.</p><p><strong>Changed GUIDs or enclosure URLs.</strong> Migrating hosts, or &quot;fixing&quot; old episodes, can regenerate identifiers. Apps then see duplicates or lose subscribers&#39; play state. Ask your host how they preserve GUIDs before migrating.</p><p><strong>Slow or redirect-heavy fetches.</strong> Every analytics prefix and tracker adds a redirect hop before the audio starts. Distribution Health&#39;s Feed Fetch Performance panel shows per-hop timing and the total; if you see several hops or a slow one, see <a href="/academy/why-multiple-podcast-trackers-are-a-recipe-for-disaster.html">why multiple trackers cause problems</a>.</p><p><strong>Media hosting that can&#39;t handle launch spikes.</strong> A host that times out during a launch produces failed downloads, not a lower rank — but the effect on new listeners is the same.</p><p><strong>Feed refresh timing.</strong> Platforms poll feeds on their own schedules, typically within hours. Publishing regularly helps <em>listeners</em> form a habit, and follows and plays are what Apple says feed popularity — but there is no documented bonus for a particular cadence. Pick a schedule you can sustain.</p><h2 id="platform-specific-notes" tabindex="-1">Platform-Specific Notes <a class="header-anchor" href="#platform-specific-notes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Platform-Specific Notes&quot;">​</a></h2><p>You publish one feed to every directory, so &quot;platform-specific optimization&quot; mostly means knowing what each platform reads.</p><p><strong>Apple Podcasts</strong> reads show name, channel name, and episode titles for search, plus popularity and engagement from search results. It does not factor ratings, reviews, or shares directly into search (though they influence listening and follows, which do count). Category and explicit flags must be correct for listing.</p><p><strong>Spotify</strong> reads titles and descriptions (first 250 characters especially), transcripts, chapters, and topic/guest tags, and encourages off-platform links to your Spotify pages.</p><p><strong>YouTube Music</strong> can ingest your RSS feed in supported regions, turning each episode into a static-image video. It does <em>not</em> sync later changes to titles, descriptions, artwork, or audio — edits have to be made in YouTube Studio. Source: <a href="https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/13525207?hl=en" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">YouTube RSS delivery</a>.</p><p><strong>Amazon Music</strong> and other directories follow Apple&#39;s requirements closely; a feed that passes Apple&#39;s checks will generally pass theirs.</p><h2 id="structured-data-belongs-on-your-website-not-in-your-feed" tabindex="-1">Structured Data Belongs on Your Website, Not in Your Feed <a class="header-anchor" href="#structured-data-belongs-on-your-website-not-in-your-feed" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Structured Data Belongs on Your Website, Not in Your Feed&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Older advice suggests adding Schema.org markup <em>inside</em> the RSS feed. Podcast apps don&#39;t read it there, and Google doesn&#39;t document any podcast rich result that comes from RSS markup.</p><p>Where structured data does help is on your <strong>website</strong>: episode pages with <code>PodcastEpisode</code> / <code>PodcastSeries</code> JSON-LD, a clear title, transcript, and show notes give Google (and AI answer engines) something to index and cite. Spotify&#39;s own SEO guidance recommends a dedicated podcast website with rich show notes for exactly this reason. Source: <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/structured-data/generate-structured-data-with-javascript" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Google: structured data guidance</a>. Treat it as web SEO that supports podcast discovery, not as a feed setting that improves in-app rankings.</p><h2 id="a-maintenance-routine" tabindex="-1">A Maintenance Routine <a class="header-anchor" href="#a-maintenance-routine" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A Maintenance Routine&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Monthly (10 minutes):</strong> open Distribution Health. Confirm the feed is Well-Formed, review any warnings, check the valid-episode count matches what you&#39;ve published, and glance at fetch timing.</p><p><strong>When you change anything about the show:</strong> re-check the show title, author, and description against your show keywords in the Planner. If you rename the show or change the author string, note the date so you can read the effect in <a href="/features/keyword-tracker.html">Keyword Tracker</a>.</p><p><strong>Before migrating hosts:</strong> confirm GUIDs and enclosure URLs will be preserved (or redirected), and set up a 301 redirect from the old feed URL.</p><p><strong>Quarterly:</strong> revisit category choice against what&#39;s charting in <a href="/features/all-charts.html">All Charts</a>, and refresh the description if your content focus has shifted.</p><h2 id="measuring-rss-optimization-success" tabindex="-1">Measuring RSS Optimization Success <a class="header-anchor" href="#measuring-rss-optimization-success" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Measuring RSS Optimization Success&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Feed work has two kinds of outcome. Requirement fixes show up as <em>presence</em>: episodes appear on every platform, on time, without duplicates — Distribution Health confirms it. Metadata changes show up as <em>positions</em>: Keyword Tracker shows whether the keywords you put in the title, author, and description moved. Keep the two apart when you evaluate what worked.</p><h2 id="put-this-into-practice" tabindex="-1">Put this into practice <a class="header-anchor" href="#put-this-into-practice" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Put this into practice&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Check <a href="/academy/why-multiple-podcast-trackers-are-a-recipe-for-disaster.html">how multiple download trackers affect your feed</a> and use the <a href="/academy/podcast-seo-clinic-series-quick-wins-troubleshooting-guide.html">SEO troubleshooting guide</a> to prioritize fixes. Follow <a href="/features/distribution-health.html">Distribution Health</a> to inspect your feed and listings in PodSEO.</p><p>Explore <a href="https://podseo.com/features/search-visibility/?utm_source=help&amp;utm_medium=referral&amp;utm_campaign=academy&amp;utm_content=rss-feed-optimization-the-technical-foundation-of-podcast-seo" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">PodSEO Search Visibility</a> to see how it supports this work. You can sign up from the product page; check <a href="https://podseo.com/pricing/?utm_source=help&amp;utm_medium=referral&amp;utm_campaign=academy&amp;utm_content=rss-feed-optimization-the-technical-foundation-of-podcast-seo" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">current plans</a> for feature availability.</p>',48)])])}const m=t(r,[["render",i]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
